{
 "id": "l07",
 "label": "7",
 "title": "Words that link and describe",
 "summary": "The ligatures -ng, -g and na that join two words when one describes the other",
 "minutes": 35,
 "goals": [
  "Choose the right ligature: -ng, -g or na",
  "Join an adjective and a noun in either order",
  "Recognise the other word sequences that need a ligature",
  "Know the exceptions where no ligature is used",
  "Repeat a word for emphasis"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "What a ligature does",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "A special feature of Tagalog is the use of **connectives** or **ligatures** to \"link together\" two words that sit side by side."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "The ligatures have **no meaning of their own**. They only show that the two words are closely related and that, generally, **one modifies the other**."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "The first word ends in…",
      "Ligature",
      "How it is added"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "a vowel",
       "[[-ng|link]]",
       "attached as a suffix to the end of the first word"
      ],
      [
       "the consonant **n**",
       "[[-g|link]]",
       "attached as a suffix to the end of the first word"
      ],
      [
       "a consonant other than n",
       "[[na|link]]",
       "a separate word placed between the two words"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "The three ligatures"
    },
    {
     "type": "pattern",
     "title": "Three recipes",
     "rows": [
      {
       "label": "After a vowel",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "malakí",
         "r": "pred"
        },
        {
         "t": "-ng",
         "r": "link"
        },
        {
         "t": "báhay",
         "r": "noun"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "label": "After n",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "mayáman",
         "r": "pred"
        },
        {
         "t": "-g",
         "r": "link"
        },
        {
         "t": "laláki",
         "r": "noun"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "label": "After another consonant",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "maásim",
         "r": "pred"
        },
        {
         "t": "na",
         "r": "link"
        },
        {
         "t": "manggà",
         "r": "noun"
        }
       ]
      }
     ],
     "note": "The ligature always comes after the first word."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "The rules with examples",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "1. -ng after a vowel"
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Ang malakíng báhay ay isáng páaralan.",
       "en": "The big house is a school.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Ang",
         "article",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "malakíng",
         "big +ng",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "báhay",
         "house",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "is",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "isáng",
         "a, one",
         "other"
        ],
        [
         "páaralan",
         "school",
         "pred"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Marunong ang bátang mabaít.",
       "en": "The good child is intelligent.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Marunong",
         "intelligent",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "ang",
         "article",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "bátang",
         "child +ng",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "mabaít",
         "good",
         "pred"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "**malaki + -ng + bahay** = big house. **batà + -ng + mabaít** = good child."
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "2. -g after n"
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Ang mayámang laláki ay mapagbigáy.",
       "en": "The rich man is generous.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Ang",
         "article",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "mayámang",
         "rich +g",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "laláki",
         "man",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "is",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "mapagbigáy",
         "generous",
         "pred"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Ang hánging malakás ay malamíg.",
       "en": "The strong wind is cold.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Ang",
         "article",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "hánging",
         "wind +g",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "malakás",
         "strong",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "is",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "malamíg",
         "cold",
         "pred"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "**mayáman + -g + laláki** = rich man. **hángin + -g + malakás** = strong wind (literally wind strong)."
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "3. na after other consonants"
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Kumáin siyá ng maásim na manggà.",
       "en": "He/She ate a sour mango.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Kumáin",
         "ate",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "siyá",
         "he/she",
         "pron"
        ],
        [
         "ng",
         "(object)",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "maásim",
         "sour",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "na",
         "ligature",
         "link"
        ],
        [
         "manggà",
         "mango",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Magandá ang tahímik na batà.",
       "en": "The quiet child is beautiful.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Magandá",
         "beautiful",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "ang",
         "article",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "tahímik",
         "quiet",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "na",
         "ligature",
         "link"
        ],
        [
         "batà",
         "child",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "**maásim + na + manggà** = sour mango. **tahímik + na + batà** = quiet child."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "rule",
     "title": "Several adjectives",
     "t": "In a series of more than one adjective before a noun, **only the last adjective** has a ligature: **mabaít at masunúring áso**."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Either order, same rule",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Changing the order of nouns, adjectives, verbs and adverbs **does not change the meaning**, and the ligature **still comes after the first word**, following the same rules."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "One order",
      "The other order",
      "Meaning"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "[[batàng babáe|noun]]",
       "[[babáeng batà|noun]]",
       "young girl"
      ],
      [
       "[[matabáng lalakí|noun]]",
       "[[lalakíng matabá|noun]]",
       "fat man"
      ],
      [
       "[[íbong lumílipad|noun]]",
       "[[lumílipad na íbon|noun]]",
       "flying bird"
      ],
      [
       "[[pagód na dumatíng|pred]]",
       "[[dumatíng na pagód|pred]]",
       "arrived tired"
      ],
      [
       "[[kumáing mabilís|pred]]",
       "[[mabilís na kumáin|pred]]",
       "ate quickly"
      ],
      [
       "[[matagál na pag-aáway|noun]]",
       "[[pag-aáway na matagál|noun]]",
       "long feud"
      ],
      [
       "[[mahál na totoó|pred]]",
       "[[totoóng mahál|pred]]",
       "truly expensive"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Swap the order, keep the meaning"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "Look at the first word",
     "t": "Take **hángin** (wind) and **malakás** (strong). If hángin comes first, it ends in n, so add -g: **hánging malakás**. If malakás comes first, it ends in s, so use na: malakás na hángin. The first word always decides."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Other word sequences that need ligatures",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Ligatures are not only for adjective + noun. These sequences also use them:"
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Sequence",
      "Example",
      "Meaning"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "1. Noun + noun",
       "Ang **batàng babáe** ay natúlog. / Ang lólo ko ay **matandàng laláki**.",
       "The young girl slept. / My grandfather is an old man."
      ],
      [
       "2. Noun + verb (or verb + noun)",
       "Ang **íbong lumílipad** ay binaríl. / Ang **lumílipad na íbon** ay binaríl.",
       "The flying bird was shot."
      ],
      [
       "3. Adjective + verb (or verb + adjective)",
       "**Pagód na dumatíng** si tátay sa báhay. / **Dumatíng na pagód** si tátay sa báhay.",
       "My father arrived home tired."
      ],
      [
       "4. Verb + adverb (or adverb + verb)",
       "**Kumáing mabilís** si Pablo. / **Mabilís na kumáin** si Pablo.",
       "Pablo ate quickly."
      ],
      [
       "5. Noun + adverb (or adverb + noun)",
       "Iwásan ang **pag-aáway na matagál**. / Iwásan ang **matagál na pag-aáway**.",
       "Avoid the long feud."
      ],
      [
       "6. Adjective + adverb (or adverb + adjective)",
       "**Mahál na totoó** ang reló mo. / **Totoóng mahál** ang reló mo.",
       "Your watch is truly expensive."
      ],
      [
       "7. Conjunction + adverb",
       "**Samantálang walâ**, mágtiis táyo. / **Sakáling dúmating**, paghintayín ang mensahéro.",
       "While [we have] none, let us be patient. / If he/she comes, make the messenger wait."
      ],
      [
       "8. Verb + verb",
       "**Hinayáang umalís** ang batà. / **Namatáy na lumalában** ang sundálo.",
       "The child was allowed to leave. / The soldier died fighting."
      ],
      [
       "9. Adverb + adverb, repeated for emphasis",
       "**Gabíng-gabí** na siyá umuwî. / **Búkas na búkas** talaga ay bábalik ako.",
       "He/She came home very late. / I shall certainly return tomorrow."
      ],
      [
       "10. Adjective + adjective, repeated as a superlative",
       "**Malínaw na malínaw** ang útos niya. / **Mabaít na mabaít** ang gurò ko.",
       "His/her command is very clear. / My teacher is very kind."
      ],
      [
       "11. Noun + pronoun (or pronoun + noun)",
       "Ang **báhay na iyán** ay ákin. / Itó ang **áking lúpa**.",
       "That house is mine. / This is my land."
      ],
      [
       "12. Pronoun + adjective (or adjective + pronoun)",
       "**Ikáw na marúnong** ay magsalitâ. / Ang **hangál na itó** ay umíiyak.",
       "You who are learned should speak. / This fool is crying."
      ],
      [
       "13. Verb + pronoun (or pronoun + verb)",
       "**Tatawáging anó** ang batà? / Táyong **kaniláng inapí** ay natúto.",
       "By what name shall the child be called? / We, whom they maltreated, learned."
      ],
      [
       "14. Pronoun + pronoun",
       "Kinúha nínyo **yaóng amín**. / **Táyong kaniláng** inapí ay natúto.",
       "You took that which is ours. / We, whom they maltreated, learned."
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Sequences with ligatures"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "english",
     "title": "Don't worry about the verbs",
     "t": "Many verbs here (dumatíng, kumáin, lumílipad) change form in later lessons. For now, just notice that the ligature follows the same rule every time."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "When there is no ligature",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Some sequences do **not** take a ligature:"
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Case",
      "Example",
      "Meaning"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "The conjunction **kung** (if)",
       "Kung walâ pa ako, máuna na kayó.",
       "If I am not around yet, you may go ahead."
      ],
      [
       "Verb + verb that makes an adjectival expression",
       "Mamatáy-mabúhay ang reló ko.",
       "My watch stops periodically. (literally: dies and lives)"
      ],
      [
       "Helping verb + main verb",
       "Áyaw kumáin ng máysakit.",
       "The sick person does not want to eat."
      ],
      [
       "Noun + possessive pronoun (ko, námin, nila…)",
       "aklát ko, báhay námin, lúpa nila",
       "my book, our house, their land"
      ],
      [
       "Adjective + nominative pronoun (ka, kayó…)",
       "Marúnong ka. / Mababaít kayó.",
       "You are intelligent (or learned). / You (plural) are kind."
      ],
      [
       "Most verb + pronoun combinations",
       "Liligáya táyo. / Kinúha ko itó.",
       "We shall be happy. / I took this."
      ],
      [
       "Pronouns in the subjunctive mood",
       "Kung akó [ay] íkaw, áalis áko.",
       "If I were you, I will leave."
      ],
      [
       "Two pronouns that form a complete sentence",
       "Kaniyá itó.",
       "This is his/hers."
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Exceptions"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "remember",
     "title": "The pattern behind the exceptions",
     "t": "Generally, the exceptions contain either a **possessive pronoun after a noun** (ko, mo, niyá, námin, nátin, ninyó, nilá) or a **nominative pronoun** that changes form in an inverted sentence (ka, kayó, kamí, táyo, siyá, silá)."
    }
   ]
  },
